  "worked_solution": "A type I region can be written as\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le x\\le b,\\,g(x)\\le y\\le f(x)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type I region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=y+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,\u27f9\\,y=x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThe function $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ is already written as a function of $x.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, we have $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nWe draw a vertical arrow through the region to determine the lower function $g(x)$ and the upper function $f(x).$ A vertical line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type I region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096470/assets/599aad21fec8f1c43c674063c34eaffa8e73857dd35cac8caba0d4a593c6a3f8.png)\n\nThe vertical line enters the region through the lower function $y=x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and leaves through the upper function $y=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$ So, we have $x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}.\n$$",
